**Read:** [Cleaning and surface properties — Mauro Taborelli](../sources/Cleaning-and-Surface-Properties-Taborelli.lingua#r_1RDARBEFXDAW9Y2DNTJ2QBEP2E), controlled drying; [Physical chemistry of interfaces and colloids — Diethelm Johannsmann](../sources/Physical-Chemistry-of-Interfaces-and-Colloids-Johannsmann.lingua#r_5FAT1MRFSVXVDS9Z6VCF4YM7TD), section 11's evaporation and film formation.

For a uniform retained liquid film of thickness h and nonvolatile solute mass concentration c, complete solvent evaporation with no solute loss leaves mass per projected area σ=c h. Drying a liquid is not the same as removing everything previously dissolved in it. Concentration, precipitation, adsorption and chemical change can alter the final distribution.

A hypothetical 10 µm film containing 1 mg/L nonvolatile solute leaves 10 µg/m² if the assumptions hold. The total solvent-removal energy also includes latent heat and heat exchange. Evaporation can cool a surface; condensation depends on the surrounding vapor partial pressure and the surface's saturation condition, not simply whether it is below room temperature.

**Work:** Derive the areal mass in SI and by directly counting liquid volume over 1 m². Compare measured average residue with a locally concentrated deposit. Draw a mass and energy boundary for drying and state where a temperature, gas-flow or humidity measurement belongs.

